## 1️⃣ Area 1: Authentication & Account Management## 📋 Testing Structure

* Dynamic Baselining: Record a standard, successful transaction pattern (e.g., successful login or profile save) to establish expected response sizes, HTTP status codes, and keys.
* Target Isolation: Target individual HTTP request properties sequentially (username first, then password, then custom parameters) to isolate the source of any changes in application behavior.
* Dual-Session Segregation: Keep active browser tabs completely separate. Use Account A strictly to generate modification links/requests, and Account B strictly to harvest session identifier data.

## 🔍 Response Verification Criteria

* SQLi / NoSQLi Confirmation: Look for structural variations in backend behavior. A vulnerability exists if inputting an injection pattern changes an error screen into a successful page redirect, shifts response length significantly, or logs you into a profile without matching valid criteria.
* Auth Bypass Confirmation: Look for state indicators in client-side applications. A vulnerability exists if altering a raw gateway response payload (e.g., forcing a false state to true) forces the browser interface to load authenticated sub-menus, even if backend APIs fail to return data.
* BOLA / IDOR Confirmation: Check data modification outcomes. A vulnerability exists if the backend replies with an HTTP 200 OK or {"status":"updated"} signature after changing an identifier code to match Account B's information, and Account B's actual physical dashboard profile updates.

